'''Anyos Federales''' ({{literally|federal years}}), also known as '''Reconstrucción''' ("reconstruction")''',''' refers to a period of [[W:Nation-building|nation-building]] in Baliscan history following the [[Compromise of 1827]] and an [[Baliscan Civil War|extended civil war]], which resulted in the country's federalization under it's first president, [[Michelangelo Valentino]]. It was during this time that the name "Balisca" was adopted by the government with the end of the [[Confederal Question]], distinguishing the [[Southern Confederation|early confederation]] from the new federal state. From this time, Balisca was seen as a single [[W:Federated state|federated state]], consisting of the four [[W:Classical republicanism|republics]] ([[Bayara]], [[Cerdanya]], [[Estuaria]] and [[Hesperia]]).

Government sponsored-programs enacted by the empowered Confederal Congress during this time resulted in an unprecedented wave of [[Immigration to Balisca|immigrants to Balisca]], resulting in the introduction of influxes of cheap labor and the influence of European capitalist intervention in Baliscan economic and social policy. Early investments in industry and the development of the railway network during the 1840s came almost exclusively from private hands. Without a central labor regulatory agencies, these construction projects were quickly realized, often at great loss of life. Actual industrialization only took off after 1850 in the wake of the railroad construction, and for the next two decades Baliscan industry grew exponentially.

In the later years of the ''Anyos Federales'', the establishment was threatened by the rise of the ''[[Cosmocazo]]'' movement, which arose as from those opposed to the displays of {{W|caesarism}} of President [[Michelangelo Valentino]] and the [[W:centralism (Peru)|centralization]] of political and economic power in [[Castejón]]. The tenure Valentino's successor, [[Josèp Bernat]] was fraught with growing radicalism, particularly amongst [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchists]] within the {{W|labor movement}}, and by Bernat's efforts to crush protests with violent force by [[Regions of Balisca|regional militias]] in the [[Cantonal War]]. After three tumultuous years in office, Bernat (operating from [[Maracaju]]) resigned on 8 June 1876, and fled to Brazil. The [[1883 Confederal Convention]] would overwhelmingly consist of the Cantonalists, and the confederation was subsequently reorganized into [[Cantons of Balisca|cantons]] and [[Communes of Balisca|communes]], and the provinces were abolished.

